WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.784
Challenging weather is any condition or combination of conditions that reduces control, visibility, endurance, sensor performance, data quality, or recovery margin.

00:00:09.784 --> 00:00:18.698
It is defined by the aircraft, crew, mission, site, and current rules, not by how dramatic the weather appears.

00:00:18.698 --> 00:00:24.568
Wind and gusts can displace a small aircraft and increase power use.

00:00:24.568 --> 00:00:30.873
Rain, snow, fog, humidity, dust, or smoke can reduce visibility and contaminate sensors.

00:00:30.873 --> 00:00:35.439
Heat and cold affect batteries, electronics, crews, and payloads.

00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:36.961
Conditions compound.

00:00:36.961 --> 00:00:48.919
Moderate wind may be manageable with a warm battery and clear recovery area, yet become unacceptable when cold, haze, difficult terrain, and weak signal occur together.

00:00:48.919 --> 00:00:52.615
Risk assessment must consider the whole system.

00:00:52.615 --> 00:00:55.877
Rapid change is itself a hazard.

00:00:55.877 --> 00:01:07.835
A mission may launch in acceptable conditions and encounter a passing shower, gust front, forming fog, falling temperature, smoke, or deteriorating visibility before collection is complete.

00:01:07.835 --> 00:01:10.661
Weather also determines mission value.

00:01:10.661 --> 00:01:19.140
Unstable footage, obscured lenses, deep haze, or poor contrast may produce data that cannot support the intended decision.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.576
Completing the route is not success when the evidence is unusable.

00:01:24.576 --> 00:01:28.272
Conservative decision-making and legal compliance come first.

00:01:28.272 --> 00:01:40.665
Follow current official information and manufacturer limitations, preserve generous margins, and delay, modify, return, land, or cancel rather than force an aircraft or crew beyond safe capability.
